{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/general-onyx-path-discussion/1521048-d20-modern-variants-vs-cofd-owod","id":1521048,"title":"D20 Modern (+Variants) vs. CofD/oWoD","path":["Home","Onyx Path","General","General Onyx Path Discussion"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-08-20T12:09:01-07:00","id":1521048,"title":"D20 Modern (+Variants) vs. CofD/oWoD","content":"Which is a better system overall? <br /> <br /> And also, how translatable is CofD/oWoD material to d20 systems?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Jade Kingdom Warrior","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25523-shakanaka","name":"Shakanaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"951"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-20T15:39:02-07:00","id":1521079,"title":"","content":"&quot;Better&quot; is very dependent on what you want out of a game. Given the massive differences in mechanics though, transferring anything more than broad concepts would be rather work intensive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-22T16:05:14-07:00","id":1521226,"title":"","content":"<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">I don\u2019t know if it counts as D20 Modern but there is Monte Cook\u2019s World of Darkness which might give an idea of how it works. <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/50229/Monte-Cooks-World-of-Darkness?affiliate_id=1664029\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product...ate_id=1664029</a></div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25049-zakrulz","name":"ZakRulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25049_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-22T18:04:02-07:00","id":1521228,"title":"","content":"I'm gonna have to take something of the opposite approach.<br /> <br /> D20's systems, because they tend to root themselves around the Dungeons and Dragons model, tend to fall into a root problem with the dice system-primarily, the issue of swinginess.<br /> <br /> One of the things about systems that center on a range of randomness is that it needs ceilings and floors to that randomization in accordance to what a character is good and not good at-you don't want to spec yourself as the best gunslinger at the scale you're introduced into only to then miss every shot and then have someone who didn't put any investment in gun shot to get a kill shot. When you build a character, you want the level of investment you put into whatever is they do is reflected.<br /> <br /> There's a couple of ways you can do this, but I'm gonna highlight how the Storyteller (and other dice pool based systems) and PbtA games do this, and then we'll use that to examine the problem with D20.<br /> <br /> Dice pool systems like Storytelling/Storyteller primarily benefit from the fact that there are a lot of levers that allow them to affect the ways in which a character succeeds and feels, primarily centered around the three main poles of a) the number of dice that make up a pool, b) the target number each die would need to get for success, and c) the number of successes needed to succeed. As a basic idea, that gives game designers a lot of ways to express what success means and feels like, but across all iterations the way a character is designed by the player is as significant a component to that state of success as the opposition laid out by other players against any dice pool used to determine the outcome. From that basic idea, you can play with that idea to feed whatever genre or success boundaries you'd like. I have a fondness for systems where the dice pool is decided by one set of determiners on the player's sheet (often the attribute), the target number is decided by another set of determiners on the player's sheet (often the skills), and the degree of successes needed mapping to the Six Answers*. Chronicles simplifies those three poles by setting the target number at 8 or higher and the total number of susccesses at 1 in order to streamline the gameplay(being really reductive about the math, basically all any player needs to reliably succeed at their actions for the base mechanic is 3-4 dice, with anything more just being insurance), but also in service of it's horror setting by making situations a scramble for any influx of dice they can get against a world that takes away any advantage you get, often reducing all talent and skill down a decent gamble, but a gamble nonetheless, and exceptional answers to conflicts(exceptional success and dramatic failures) being the result of either exceptional (mis-/)luck or overwhelming (dis/)advantage. In all cases, though, the character and it's representational ability to have things happen in alignment with them comes first, and that ability keeps success and grounded in their excellence or deficiencies in the themes of the story, with negatives being rooted in the world acting against them. And this approach is generally pretty solid, because so long as the principles aren't wildly mismatched with each other, you can have pretty near infinite expansion all the poles, which makes it useful for long running games.<br /> <br /> PbtA plays a little bit closer to D20 by being [Number from 2d6]+[Stat modifer], but it's rigidity works better on account of being <i>really rigid</i>, but also having fluid success states the norm, typically settling on results of 6 and lower being a miss/fail, 7-9 being a hit/success with a complication (Yes But), and 10 and higher being a solid success (and sometimes even including exceptional results). On just the 2d6, that means a little over half of your rolls will succeed(58.3%, nearly 3 out of 5), but only about 3 out of 20)16.65%, to be exact) will be uncomplicated. But character design in PbtA tends to emphasize that every character has an exceptional Stat, with the max being 3 and being fairly easy to start out of the gate with. Running with that number, that means you can get a full success on 7 and higher (that 58.3%), a complicated success at 4(which means your character is doing the thing you built them to do 91.62% of the time, even if 33.32 of all your rolls is a complicated one), and only failing to do what you set out to do 8.38% of the time. Forwards, Hold, and Ongoing modifiers only make the more potent, and even 1's and 2s as either your stat modifier or add ons to it feel significant, because every +1 swings a huge percentage of the probability your way until you get the the minute details. Some PbtA games, particular if they go for a genre where disempowerment is part of the feel ,can counter this by including negatives or playing with the success framing. All modifiers are pretty hard to swing into play, coming from limited locations, so it's hard to rob a player of their sense of competency or incompenntcy, and exceptions feel exceptional to the circumstances of the story. As a general rule, neither the dice roll or the stats in PbtA that a character bring to it feels insignificant-each plays a vital roll and makes a significant contribution to the gameplay. The primary problem with PbtA games is that they tend to be a little more limited in terms of time scale, because regardless of the rate of growth, the ceilings and floors of those games are really apparent and obvious, partly as a way to avoid the D20 problem, so pretty much any game can only go on so long.<br /> <br /> So, with our comparisons set up, back to d20 swinginess.<br /> <br /> The d20 itself is already a much larger degree of variability than our other dice (10 and 11 numbers versus 20 before accounting for target numbers), and it doesn't help that most d20 games consider a shifting target value a perk rather than a problem**, but the bigger problem is that it has no real standard for growth or game length in addition to that. So what happens in a lot d20 games is that, at the beginning, the d20 is the major source of modifiers, which makes if you can do something or not an entire crap shoot-you can be as good at swinging that sword as you like, that d20 is what actually determines your ability. By contrast, late games in d20 then to have players with modifiers so large that it renders the d20 irrelevant-even if you roll a 1, you entire christmas tree's worth of modifiers can often make hittting the target number a breeze. This wouldn't be a problem if games identified the middle ground where the balance of modifiers and die roll kept character design relevant without invalidating the die roll and visa versa, but so many d20 games just build along the same level of start and progression as the games that came before it do, and thus end up continuing the same mistake***.and arguably, why would they, it's confusing to have a game where the most common non-character modifier for starting characters is 3, when you can just start with a system where modifiers of 1 make more sense for the setting. And because these games includes a tradition of long running games with theoretically exponential modifier growth, it all too often falls into the end game trap even if they try to start with a better balance.<br /> <br /> There's a lot of other problems with d20, particularly since a lot of games continue on Dungeons and Dragons gaming-philosophy-and-theming-as-mechanics with no regard to whether it plays well with the genre and setting they have (or if indeed it reflects a worldview they want to support, that's really getting into the weeds), but honestly just at it's basic core, d20 is built to be largely unpleasant, and few d20 games take the time to design in such a way to resolve it's core problems in a way that also works for the fiction.<br /> <br /> So, uh, yeah, going to answer the questions, Chronicles and World have better systems inherently, and there's no good reason porting them over to d20 systems.<br /> <br /> *For those who don't know, the Six Answers is a literary tool for conflict scenes, where in any conflict that has uncertainty has one of six answers, centered on whether they succeed or not (Yes and No) and the unintended/unexpected outcomes of that success or failure (effectively expressed as And, But, or None), leading to an array of Yes, Yes But, Yes And, No, No But, and No And. Yes and No are probably easy to figure out, so as a quick way to grasp But, And, and None, think of critical failures and success as being the province of And answers (you do the thing and something extra happens, or you don't do the thing and it's worse than initially expected, None are straight successes and failures (you either do or don't do the thing and nothing else comes with it, and Buts are whenever a mechanic has a catch that plays opposite to the success state (&quot;you fail to make the shot, but you do skim them so they bleed and allow you to follow them&quot; and &quot;you make the shot, but only because you got hyperfocused on making it, and so didn't realize you sprained your ankle when you fought to get in position for the shot, so good luck following them&quot;, as examples).<br /> **Say what you will for DnD 4E, it's Player Strategy Guide sets a standard for hitting targets in combat at hitting about 60%-65% of the time, a guide of success literally no other game edition has listed, and is really useful for backtracking the rate of progression, or bullshiting the numbers at least. That one piece of information is about the only reason I can rate any playability in d20 games of any sort.<br /> ***Both DnD 3rd and 5th Edition have had players identify that levels 7 through 14 is where the game hits the particular sweet spot of actually working, and it's notable that both of those editions have the same sweet spot problem, as an example.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-27T02:47:22-07:00","id":1521457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZakRulz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/general-onyx-path-discussion/1521048-d20-modern-variants-vs-cofd-owod?p=1521226#post1521226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">I don\u2019t know if it counts as D20 Modern but there is Monte Cook\u2019s World of Darkness which might give an idea of how it works. <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/50229/Monte-Cooks-World-of-Darkness?affiliate_id=1664029\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product...ate_id=1664029</a></div> </div> </div> </div> </div>That book is how I first got into WoD and CoD properties. For me at least, as a gamer whose first experiences were in d20 games, dice pool systems can still pose a challenge. I spend a bit of my free time making d20 setting hacks for a number of WoD and CoD games as a mental exercise to better understand and translate concepts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12074-robert-d","name":"Robert D","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-27T02:53:58-07:00","id":1521458,"title":"","content":"There's a new d20 game that got released last fall called Everyday Heroes. A spiritual successor to D20 Modern, but from an independent game company called Evil Genius Games. I think it would be a good baseline if you wanted to run a d20 based game of urban horror. Its design does work to address some of the concerns about the system, especially the point ArcaneArts made about D&amp;D 3 and 5 having sweet spot of levels 7-14.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12074-robert-d","name":"Robert D","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}